  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Annie Henrietta, Lady Yule, was a British film financier whose family wealth came from Indian jute businesses. Widowed after the death of Sir David Yule, she co-founded British National Films with J. Arthur Rank and John Corfield. She also helped finance Pinewood Studios and backed a varied programme of British cinema.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Heritage records, contemporary history and studio documentation establish the family origin and film career."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S4",
      "S5",
      "S10"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Annie Henrietta, Lady Yule, was a British film financier whose family wealth came from Indian jute businesses. Widowed after the death of Sir David Yule, she co-founded British National Films with J. Arthur Rank and John Corfield. She also helped finance Pinewood Studios and backed a varied programme of British cinema.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A visit to Crabbet Stud changed Lady Yule’s plans for breeding horses. In 1925, she bought Razina, an Arabian mare already in foal to a Thoroughbred. Although initially interested in crossbred horses, Yule used Razina to establish a purebred Arabian programme at Hanstead, working alongside her daughter, Gladys.\n\nExchanges with Crabbet subsequently brought new mares and stallions into the stud. Raktha, one of Razina’s sons, went the other way, becoming an influential sire at Crabbet. Hanstead developed a reputation in the show ring, and its bloodlines spread through later breeding programmes. Gladys took over the horses in 1946 and continued the enterprise.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Detailed specialist history and stud records support the breeding decision, exchanges and succession. Later daughter-led achievements remain distinct."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S6",
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 102,
    "html": "<p>A visit to Crabbet Stud changed Lady Yule’s plans for breeding horses. In 1925, she bought Razina, an Arabian mare already in foal to a Thoroughbred. Although initially interested in crossbred horses, Yule used Razina to establish a purebred Arabian programme at Hanstead, working alongside her daughter, Gladys.</p>\r\n<p>Exchanges with Crabbet subsequently brought new mares and stallions into the stud. Raktha, one of Razina’s sons, went the other way, becoming an influential sire at Crabbet. Hanstead developed a reputation in the show ring, and its bloodlines spread through later breeding programmes. Gladys took over the horses in 1946 and continued the enterprise.</p>\r\n"
  },
